{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-103081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The derivative of $y=x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-103081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-103081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-103081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-103081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "1-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-103081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that $(\\frac{12}{3})$ means the same thing as ${}_{12}{C}_{3}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "110"] ["math" "1\\,320"] ["math" "220"] ["math" "240"] ["math" "440"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-84994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "220"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/56433bbb908fde0c0a46f68665b9ac22360c00978238124b6fac9022576b3620.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c.$ What is the value of $c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$ Therefore, $c=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-27442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-7$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-113525" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/ee92f92a52d8ade2052a62a5c19906cd86027b47f22ec986a740b9379e66d8d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{8})}=3\\sqrt{7}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-124796" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\sqrt{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)={(5-3x)}^{4},$ find the equation of the tangent at point $(2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=5-3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-12(5-3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =-12{(5-3(2))}^{3} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,1)$ is $m=12.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =12(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =12x-24 \\\\ y & =12x-23\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-12x+25"] ["math" "y=-3x+7"] ["math" "y=-4x+9"] ["math" "y=12x-23"] ["math" "y=4x-7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=12x-23"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/91f073fa77aab423ee33902451e61ad9f033b61b2a875b66ff69708d4361e24f.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that in each of the four given graphs, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. Therefore, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, none of them correspond to an invertible function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-36905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{d-4}={243}^{d+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $243={3}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{d-4}={({3}^{5})}^{d+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(d-4)}={3}^{5(d+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-4) & =5(d+3) \\\\ 2d-8 & =5d+15 \\\\ -3d & =23 \\\\ d & =-\\frac{23}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{23}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{23}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{32}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-100462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{23}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] ["text" "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] ["text" "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] ["text" "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] ["text" "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-49234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/5e350d9b4db36831eac3ff0708a2acbc903c07265d4c6709632fb8a854121747.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=2$ and $x=5.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is also a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=5,$ this root is also a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+1)(x-2)^{5}(x-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=(x+1)(x-2)^{2}(x-5)"] ["math" "y=(x+1)(x-2)^{3}(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "y=(x+1)(x-2)^{5}(x-5)"] ["math" "y=(x+1)^{2}(x-2)(x-5)^{2}"] ["math" "y=(x+1)^{3}(x-2)(x-5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-80350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=(x+1)(x-2)^{5}(x-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f51f3239c5f5cbbb69cd7c02eeebc3723d5a5f72e4cefc82fffac296b1b030a"} {"question" "q-48081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "751933f286085810e2622f697991d8bae2914a9fb3c691b74e084071556bd2f8"} {"question" "q-48081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "cat